NAME
    fetch - fetch a URL over HTTP or HTTPS

SYNOPSIS
    fetch [-o FILE] URL

DESCRIPTION
    Fetches URL and prints the response body. Both http:// and
    https:// work; https:// runs a TLS 1.2 handshake through the
    system's TLS client, with SNI from the host name. A dotted-quad
    host is used as-is, anything else resolves through /etc/hosts
    first, then DNS (nameservers from /etc/resolv.conf, default
    1.1.1.1). Up to five redirects are followed.

    The request is HTTP/1.0 with Connection: close, so the body runs
    to the server's close. Status codes outside 2xx are reported on
    standard error but the body is still printed.

    Encryption is real but trust is not verified: the server's
    certificate chain is not checked against a trust store (the TLS
    library leaves that TODO), so https:// here proves little about
    who is on the other end.

OPTIONS
    -o FILE    write the body to FILE instead of standard output

EXAMPLES
    fetch http://example.com/
    fetch https://example.com/ -o /tmp/page.html

FILES
    /etc/hosts        static names, consulted before DNS
    /etc/resolv.conf  nameservers, one per line

EXIT STATUS
    0 when the transfer completed, 1 on a bad URL, a DNS, TCP or TLS
    failure, or a truncated response.

SEE ALSO
    ping(1)
